get.description:
Executes the given SPARQL query and returns matching entities. Always request 'format=json'. Keep queries scoped with a LIMIT clause (10 or fewer) to avoid large, slow responses. Two common patterns:

1) Shared actor between two films (property P161 = 'cast member'):
SELECT ?actorLabel WHERE {
  ?film1 rdfs:label "Film Title One"@en.
  ?film2 rdfs:label "Film Title Two"@en.
  ?film1 wdt:P161 ?actor.
  ?film2 wdt:P161 ?actor.
  SERVICE wikibase:label { bd:serviceParam wikibase:language "en". }
} LIMIT 10

2) Other works by a person in a SPECIFIC role (e.g. director, property P57):
SELECT ?workLabel WHERE {
  ?person rdfs:label "Person Name"@en.
  ?work wdt:P57 ?person.
  SERVICE wikibase:label { bd:serviceParam wikibase:language "en". }
} LIMIT 10

Substitute the quoted labels with the actual entity names from the user's question.

IMPORTANT — pattern matching is about the RELATIONSHIP, not just the entity count: a question involving two named entities does not automatically fit one of these patterns. Pattern 1 only fits 'shared actor/cast member' questions. Pattern 2 only fits 'other works in the SAME role' questions (e.g. other films by the same director) — the property (P57 for director) must match the specific role asked about. A question that mixes two different relationship types for two different people (e.g. 'who composed the score for Film X, and did that same person direct Film Y') does NOT fit either pattern, even though it names two films — do not substitute a different property (like P86 for composer) into pattern 2 and treat it as a fit. If the relationship type in the question doesn't cleanly match one of the two patterns above, do not attempt a modified or best-guess query — fall back to the Wikipedia summary tool instead.

